A||||18||AutoXer||locking lug nuts||||4||
Z||000000||AutoXer||09-30-2001||02:51 PM||||How do you get the keyed lug nuts off if you lost the key?||||reg||4
Z||000001||maryjane||09-30-2001||03:16 PM||||If you are talking about the locking nuts that come on the stock Fieros, most tire shops have a socket to fit. If its the ones with an actual key, I don't have a clue. I do have a set of sockets that I got at a garage sale that are supposed to remove locking nuts of various types. Never used them, would have to dig them out to even see what they look like. Was going to sell them at my last yard sale but was afraid someone would use them unscruplessly.(sp)||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowfunds||09-30-2001||04:33 PM||||Air Chisel  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> An air chisel will split the lug in a few seconds. Had to do it to a car of mine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||09-30-2001||04:46 PM||||There was a key for the wheels on my GT that I was very fortunate to find in the pocket between the seats. Came in handy when I did the alternator work.<P>Call Pontiac or a GM dealer near you. Sure they could hook you up with one for your Fiero, or one that would work.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||09-30-2001||05:38 PM||||The locking lug nut removers have some sort of sharp edges in them to grip the lug nut.  Once you get the offending lug nut off, throw the rest away.  Nobody is going to remove your wheels.  Much easier to steal the entire car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||tomanyfieros||09-30-2001||06:29 PM||||if ur lucky like me, weld a lug nut on to the locking one and turn ur new one.... works everytime! hope this helps... THanx Jason<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dreamer.gif">    <BR><B>We all have dreams, but not everyone gets them...</B>||||reg||1
Z||000006||AutoXer||09-30-2001||06:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Once you get the offending lug nut off, throw the rest away.  [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Absolutely!!<P>The problem is I have been saying that for about a year now and I lost the tool/key before I actually replaced them!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Thanks for all the advise guys. (non gender specific use of the word guys)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jefrysuko||09-30-2001||07:06 PM||||If you don't have access to any of the resources mentioned above you can try what I did.  Take a Dremel and get a dimond tiped bit.  Now spend all day grinding at the inside of the lugnut (it extends past the stud 1/2" and the hole goes all the way through) to make it into a square so that you can fit a square socket extension into it and use that to turn it.  I would highly recomend any other method over this but that was my only option.<BR>Jeffrey||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||09-30-2001||08:26 PM||||Go buy a cheap socket that just barely doesn't fit. Then hammer the damn thing on, and use as normal. Use a drift in the middle to remove it once it's off, repeat...<P>Warning: You may go through a couple of sockets this way...||||reg||7
Z||000009||Raydar||09-30-2001||09:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Go buy a cheap socket that just barely doesn't fit. Then hammer the damn thing on, and use as normal. Use a drift in the middle to remove it once it's off, repeat...<P>Warning: You may go through a couple of sockets this way...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is the same way I got mine off. Used the same socket for all four of them. Hammered it on with a BFH. The locks were so cheesy that they actually deformed. Didn't even scratch the socket. One of the easiest things I've ever done.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||14
Z||000010||PBJ||09-30-2001||09:19 PM||||If it is the lock wheel nut that accepts the plastic caps the screw on to cover them like the other wheel nuts than get a 12 point 20mm socket and pound it on.  Has worked every time for me at work.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fleabitdog||09-30-2001||09:24 PM||||I bought a special socket from the sanp on man for locknuts on Fiero's. It cost me $20 but I have used it at least ten times so far. It is completely smooth on the inside. You just pound it on the locknut with a hammer, use about a 3" extension on a 1/2" drive breakerbar or ratchet and loosen the nut. Once it is loose knock the socket until it falls off the lugnut. The locknuts are not damaged at all. You should be able to get the socket off any tool truck, but make sure you fit it to the nut before you buy it. Also Mach10 was correct in his suggestion. I beleive it was a smaller diameter 12 point socket that will work. Good luck  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000012||AutoXer||10-01-2001||09:20 PM||||Thanks everyone.<P>I used the socket and the BFH method and it worked great.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||10-01-2001||11:12 PM||||Thanks for this wonderful advise guys!<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">    Now I have to figure out how to get into RossT's garage without his knowledge and get his gold rims off of his 88. <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif">    This is a job for <B><I>"Stuper Man!"</I></B><P>Just Kidding Ross!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>This thread is just about as good as how to get into your locked Fiero through the sunroof a couple months back.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 10-01-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000014||Cheever3000||10-02-2001||02:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I beleive it was a smaller diameter 12 point socket that will work.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I would think so!  20mm is huge, isn't it?  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Master_Sushi||10-02-2001||11:19 AM||||Not to worry. I don't think anyone would waste their time steeling stock fiero wheels  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Anyone that knows what they are doing can get around locking lug nuts anyway.||||reg||1
